// Shared build configuration, committed to the repo. Pulls in a
// contributor's local, git-ignored settings (signing team, etc.) if present.
// The `?` makes the include optional, so a missing Local.xcconfig is not an
// error; see Local.xcconfig.sample for what goes in it.
#include? "Local.xcconfig"

// Apple Silicon only, permanently. There's no plan to ever build an x86_64 slice. Without this, Release/Archive builds (which don't have ONLY_ACTIVE_ARCH) would try to link x86_64 against an arm64-only libruby-static.a and fail.
ARCHS = arm64

// Embedded CRuby. Built by scripts/build-cruby.rb into Vendor/CRuby/, which is git-ignored; see docs/embedding-cruby.md. The RubyCutsVM framework target's Run Script build phase fails fast with a pointer to that script if this is missing.
//
// scripts/build-cruby.rb installs one complete header tree and one static lib per SDK, under Vendor/CRuby/{include,lib}/<platform>/, where <platform> is exactly Xcode's PLATFORM_NAME (macosx, iphoneos, iphonesimulator), so both paths below key off $(PLATFORM_NAME) instead of enumerating SDKs.
CRUBY_DIR = $(SRCROOT)/Vendor/CRuby
// SYSTEM_ rather than plain HEADER_SEARCH_PATHS: -isystem marks CRuby's headers as third-party, so clang stops reporting their doc-comment and prototype warnings in our build. Warnings in our own rubycuts_shim.c still show.
SYSTEM_HEADER_SEARCH_PATHS = $(inherited) $(CRUBY_DIR)/include/$(PLATFORM_NAME)

// Linking CRuby happens only in the RubyCutsVM framework target (OTHER_LDFLAGS there: -force_load of libruby-static.a, plus -lz for the statically linked zlib ext and CoreFoundation for the CFString calls in file.c, which the app used to pull in implicitly through Swift), which the app embeds and the widget extension links, so the 45 MB library ships once. Config/RubyCutsVM.exp exports only the rubycuts_* shim functions, letting the linker dead-strip whatever of CRuby the shim can't reach, as it did when CRuby was linked into the app.

// Gem installation UI (Settings > Gems, and the Reference line pointing to it). Delete this line to build without it, the fallback if App Review rejects downloadable gems under guideline 2.5.2. A build-time switch on purpose: guideline 2.3.1(a) requires every feature to be described in the review notes and reachable during review, so gems can't be switched on remotely after approval. Only the entry points are gated; the gem code itself stays compiled, so its tests run in both configurations.
SWIFT_ACTIVE_COMPILATION_CONDITIONS = $(inherited) RUBYCUTS_GEMS

// The app's bundle identifier. The widget extension's is derived from it ($(APP_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER).Widget), because an extension's identifier has to start with its app's. Override this one, not PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER, to build a differently identified copy (see the install:device task in mise.toml); a command-line PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER would give the extension the app's identifier too.
APP_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER = dev.stol.RubyCuts

// The version, shared by the app and the widget extension (App Store Connect flags an extension whose version differs from its app's) and by the iOS and macOS builds. Change it here, not in Xcode's General tab: that writes a per-target setting that would override this one.
MARKETING_VERSION = 1.1.0
// The build number: one counter for both platforms, so iOS and macOS uploads of the same release get the same number. It never goes back down, not even for a new MARKETING_VERSION, because the Mac App Store requires build numbers to increase across versions. Bump it with `mise run bump:build` before releasing.
CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION = 100
